      "role": "user",
      "content": "For wave pattern Q, calculate the frequency of the wave if the period is given as T = 0,02 s. (3 marks)"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student asks for wave frequency given period. Strategy: analogy_real_world. Relate frequency to how many cycles happen per second (like a runner completing laps on a track). Ask for the formula linking frequency and period. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"1 / 0.02\") -> 50.0]",
      "content": "Think of frequency like a runner completing laps on an athletics track: if you want to know how many laps the runner completes in *one second*, you look at the time taken for a single lap (the period).\n\nWhat is the formula that links frequency ($f$) and period ($T$)? Give that a try!"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "f = 1 * 0,02 = 0,02 Hz"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student made an arithmetic/operation slip, multiplying instead of dividing, resulting in 0,02. Point out the operation error and guide them to division. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"1 / 0.02\") -> 50.0]",
      "content": "Notice that you multiplied 1 by $0,02\\text{ s}$. Because period is the time *for one wave*, to find how many waves happen in *one full second*, we need to divide 1 second by that period.\n\nTry calculating $\\frac{1}{0,02}$ again, and make sure your unit is correct for frequency."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Ah, f = 1 / 0,02 = 50 Hz"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Correct calculation and unit. Finalize with clear answer, formula breakdown, and exam tip.",
      "content": "Yes, dividing 1 by the period gives $50\\text{ Hz}$:\n$$f = \\frac{1}{T} = \\frac{1}{0,02} = 50\\text{ Hz}$$\n\n**Final Answer:** $50\\text{ Hz}$ (or $50\\text{ s}^{-1}$)\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $f = \\frac{1}{T}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $f$ is the frequency (number of complete waves passing a fixed point per second, measured in Hertz or $\\text{Hz}$)\n  * $T$ is the period (time taken for *one* complete wave to pass a fixed point, measured in seconds)\n* **Why we use it:** Frequency and period are inversely proportional. If a wave takes a very short time to pass ($T$ is small), many waves pass every second ($f$ is large).\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always remember to include your correct SI units ($\text{Hz}$ or $\text{s}^{-1}$) for frequency, otherwise you will lose the final mark even if your calculation is right!"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "analogy_real_world",
    "final_answer": "50 Hz",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nT = 0.02\nf = 1 / T\nprint(f\"{int(f)} Hz\")",
    "techniques": [
      "analogy_real_world",
      "formula_application"
    ]
  }
}

