    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student wants to find the wave speed given frequency and wavelength. Following faded_example strategy, I will prompt the student to state or recall the core wave equation first before attempting the substitution. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"50 * 0.4\") -> 20.0]",
      "content": "To calculate the speed of a wave, we use the wave equation that links speed, frequency, and wavelength. What is the formula that connects speed ($v$), frequency ($f$), and wavelength ($\\lambda$)?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I used $v = \\frac{f}{\\lambda}$ so $v = 50 / 0,4 = 125\\text{ m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-1}$."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner made a common conceptual error, dividing frequency by wavelength instead of multiplying them. I need to correct this gently without unearned praise, show the correct substitution, and provide the required final breakdown and exam tip. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"50 * 0.4\") -> 20.0]",
      "content": "The wave equation actually multiplies frequency and wavelength, rather than dividing them.\n\n$$v = f \\times \\lambda$$\n$$v = 50 \\times 0,4 = 20\\text{ m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-1}$|\n\n**Final Answer:** $20\\text{ m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-1}$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $v = f \\times \\lambda$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $v$: Wave speed (how fast the wave travels per second, measured in metres per second, $\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-1}$)\n  * $f$: Frequency (the number of complete wave cycles passing a point per second, measured in Hertz, $\\text{Hz}$)\n  * $\\lambda$: Wavelength (the distance between two successive identical points in phase, measured in metres, $\\text{m}$)\n* **Why we use it:** Speed is distance over time. Since a wave travels one wavelength $\\lambda$ in the time of one period $T$ (where $f = \\frac{1}{T}$), multiplying frequency by wavelength gives the total distance the wave profile covers per second.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always double-check your formula sheet and ensure your units are in SI units before substituting values. Forgetting or swapping multiplication with division is a common lost mark!"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "faded_example",
    "final_answer": "20 m.s^-1",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nf = sp.Rational(50)\nlam = sp.Rational(4, 10)\nv = f * lam\nprint(f\"{v} m.s^-1\")",
